<DIV align="left" style="margin-left: 0%; margin-right: 0%; text-indent: 4%; font-size: 10pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times; color: #000000; background: #FFFFFF">
    We believe that the hospitality industry is poised for continued
    growth because the supply of new hotel rooms has not kept pace
    with increasing room demand, indicating that RevPAR should
    continue to increase. From 2004 through 2007, new hotel room
    supply grew by 0.4% in 2004, decreased by 0.1% in 2005,
    increased by 0.2% in 2006, and increased by 1.3% in 2007. During
    that same period, demand for hotel rooms  increased by 4.0% in
    2004, 2.8% in 2005, 0.5% in 2006, and 1.1% in 2007. From 2004
    through 2007, demand increased at an average annual rate of
    2.1%, while supply increased at an average annual rate of 0.5%.
    In other words, the demand-supply imbalance widened in favor of
    demand. This supply-demand imbalance has resulted in the strong
    growth in RevPAR rates. Even if the future demand for hotel
    rooms were to slow or flatten, we believe that the current
    supply-demand imbalance will exist for at least the next two
    years due to the lack of development of new hotel rooms as a
    result of increasing construction costs and decreased financing
    availability.
</DIV>

