<DIV align="left" style="margin-left: 0%; margin-right: 0%; text-indent: 4%; font-size: 10pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times; color: #000000; background: #FFFFFF">
    As indicated in the chart below, construction costs have
    increased an average of 8.3% a year from 2004 through 2007, and
    it does not appear that this increased demand for new rooms will
    be met by new construction in the immediate future. Furthermore,
    the difficulty of attaining financing for new developments will
    likely eliminate some of the already-proposed hotel developments
    as well as reduce the number of prospective new developments
    that would be proposed if the credit markets were more stable.
    As lending standards tighten and construction costs rise, we
    expect that the supply-demand imbalance in the hospitality
    industry will remain for the foreseeable future. In the event
    that the United States enters a period of relative economic
    slowdown, this supply-demand imbalance may become less
    pronounced in favor of demand, but we expect demand will still
    remain strong relative to supply due to the trends discussed
    above. Therefore, we believe the hospitality industry is
    well-positioned to endure a mild recession. We expect
    capitalization rates to rise during any such period of economic
    slowdown, in which case we should experience good buying
    opportunities.
</DIV>

